      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Fix Codex fork identity and cache invalidation",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- prefer `session_meta.payload.id` as the canonical Codex leaf-session identifier before parent-oriented `session_id` aliases\n- persist the parent-session file fingerprint used for a fork baseline and invalidate a warm child cache entry whenever that parent appears or changes\n- regenerate the Codex parser hash and add cold-scan plus warm-cache regression coverage\n\n## Why\n\nNewer Codex subagent logs can contain a unique child ID in `payload.id` while `payload.session_id` and `payload.forked_from_id` both point to the parent. Preferring `session_id` aliases multiple child files to the same parent identity.\n\nThere is also a separate warm-cache dependency problem: fork accounting depends on the parent snapshot at the fork timestamp, but cache freshness previously tracked only the child file. If the parent file was missing, discovered later, or appended after the child was cached, the stale child total could continue to include inherited usage.\n\nThis complements the existing fork-baseline and intra-file containment work by fixing cross-file identity and cache dependency invalidation. Refs #2037.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `swift test --filter 'CostUsageScannerBreakdownTests|CostUsageCacheTests'` — 89 tests passed\n- `make check` — passed\n- `./Scripts/compile_and_run.sh` — passed; release app built, signed, and launched\n- `make test` — the changed scanner/cache suites pass, but the full shard run still hits two unrelated `AdaptiveRefreshTimerTests` timing failures. Both reproduce in isolation after stopping CodexBar; no timer code is changed here.\n",
        "branch": "agent/fix-codex-fork-cache-baselines",
